WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.260
Hard-coded API keys are easy to create and hard to control.

2
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:08.680
The first version of a script works immediately because the value is right there.

3
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:21.340
The long-term cost appears later, when the script is committed to a repository, copied into another project, pasted into a ticket, backed up, captured in a screenshot, or included in a build artifact.

4
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.960
At that point the key has spread beyond the current file.

5
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.000
Removing the key from the current script is not complete cleanup.

6
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:40.460
Older copies may remain in repository history, archived workspaces, old branches, logs, backups, support cases, screenshots, or developer machines.

7
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:48.080
If the key ever had meaningful access, cleanup usually requires rotation or revocation, not only an edit.

8
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:54.420
Treat a hard-coded API key as exposed until the team has reason to believe the old value is no longer useful.

9
00:00:54.700 --> 00:01:01.660
Defensive Bash scripts should avoid storing API keys directly in script files or informal shared locations.

10
00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:13.840
The key should come from an approved secret-management system, platform identity, workload identity, CI/CD secret variable, protected runtime injection mechanism, or another controlled channel.

11
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:20.420
This makes the script easier to review and makes key replacement possible without editing many copies of the script.

12
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:28.540
Sometimes a local sensitive file is unavoidable, such as a client configuration file or a restricted token file required by a tool.

13
00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:34.880
That file should have strict permissions, clear ownership, a controlled location, and a defined lifecycle.

14
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:39.460
It should not live in a shared folder or broad workspace by accident.

15
00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:45.080
If the key must be replaced tomorrow, the team should know where it is used and how to update it cleanly.

16
00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:52.620
A permitted local key file has strict permissions, clear ownership, a controlled location, and a defined lifecycle.
